ain  enough information to prove only one  person is responsible defense told the  jury there is no link between the online  extortionist and cobin arguing the  evidence shows other people were using  the facebook accounts plus some of the  devices showing communication with  amanda could have come into cobin's  possession because he was a computer  repairman  justice devlin warned the jury amanda's  death is not part of this trial cobin  has pleaded not guilty to all five  charges including possession of child  pornography extortion and criminal  harassment pharah  romina daya in new westminster bc thank  you romina  one day after a jury hit right-wing  conspiracy theorist and infowars host  alex jones with a 4 million defamation  judgment the jurors have decided that  he'll also have to pay punitive damages  as well in the amount of more than 45  million dollars the money is being  awarded to the parents of a six-year-old  boy killed in the sandy hook massacre  who sued jones for claiming the school  shooting was a hoax the jury heard  expert testimony and analysis about the  value of the web of media organizations  and shell companies controlled by jones  estimated at between 135 and 270 million  dollars  help wanted ahead what some employers  are doing when they can't find a human  to hire  despite a larger than expected number of  jobs lost the overall unemployment rate  across canada remains at a record low  4.9 percent now it's great news for  workers but it can make things more  challenging for businesses as ann  gaviola explains the pandemic has paved  the way for automation in a job market  where employers are struggling to find  workers  there are more than one million job  vacancies across canada right now and  good luck to employers trying to find  workers to fill them everybody's facing  the same challenge these days  part of it is obviously compensation but  part of it is also how do we treat our  employees labor experts say prolonged  staffing shortages and covid policies  are only adding to the case for  increasing automation in the workforce  after all robots don't call in sick  don't spread sickness and don't need  rest  like this robot barista in toronto's  kensington market neighborhood you might  mistake this for vending machine coffee  but it's made on demand from beans to  cup in under two minutes fully  contactless and open 24 7.  it's good i'm no match expert but it's  cold and it's refreshing what started  with one location in 2020 has now become  five i think this is  kind of  where we're going as a society i have  mixed feelings about a robot from a jobs  perspective the makers of these robot  barista cafes say they're job creators  not job killers there's the whole back  end system that we have in place that  has a whole lot of people running it  and developing it their cost savings  comes from low overhead and they plan to  expand across canada and the us we're  getting a tremendous  interest especially in the u.s  and also here with the universities in  canada where the labor market is so  tight that they just cannot get the  people in to actually get the jobs  filled with automation accelerating  because of the pandemic income  inequality concerns also increase with  lower income earners likely most at risk  we are embarking into a new type of  economy that we're not prepared to  regulate or manage and i don't think our  societies are ready for that but that is  the tidal wave of change that is coming  in this coming era he predicts that  tidal wave of change could unfold over  the next five to 10 years whether we're  ready for it is a whole other matter ann  gaviola global news toronto  up next the new italian trattoria aiming  to be the cat's meow in new york city  new york is always a foodie's delight  boasting some of the best restaurants in  the world well a fancy new pop-up is  offering quite the unique feast as  abigail beeman reports the italian style  tracheria was inspired by some of 